Natural gas is valued using regional physical and financial benchmarks—most notably Henry Hub in North America, TTF in Europe, and JKM in Asia—which reflect local pipeline constraints, storage levels, and liquefied natural gas (LNG) trade economics. Unlike crude oil, which trades on a unified global price curve, natural gas remains regionally fragmented because moving it across oceans requires expensive liquefaction and specialized shipping. [1, 2]
Core Global Benchmarks
The table below outlines the primary pricing hubs used to value natural gas and LNG contracts globally. [1, 2]
