Pricing Mechanisms & Valuation Layers
Depending on where the gas sits in the value chain and how it is contracted, traders and portfolio managers apply specific valuation methods:
Spot vs. Futures Curves. Spot prices reflect immediate physical delivery (next-day or balance-of-month), while futures contracts on exchanges like ICE and CME lock in forward prices across seasonal winter/summer strips. [1, 2]
Basis Differentials. Regional hubs are valued by adding or subtracting a "basis" spread from a major benchmark. This spread captures pipeline bottlenecks, regional weather anomalies, and local storage capacity. [1, 2, 3]
Oil-Indexed Formulas. Many legacy long-term LNG contracts in Asia still link gas prices to a basket of crude oil imports—such as the Japan Crude Cocktail (JCC)—using a set slope (typically 10% to 15% of crude oil's price) plus a fixed constant. [1]
Netback & Export Parity. For U.S. LNG exports, valuation relies on calculating the spread between Henry Hub and international benchmarks (TTF or JKM), minus liquefaction fees, shipping charter rates, and regasification costs. [1, 2]
Value Chain Pricing Tiers
In domestic markets like the United States, the price of natural gas changes depending on how far it has traveled through the infrastructure network: [1]
Wellhead Price. The raw value of unprocessed gas at the production site before gathering and processing fees.
Citygate Price. The wholesale price paid by local distribution utilities when gas arrives at a metropolitan distribution hub.
End-Use Rates. The retail price billed to industrial, commercial, electric power, and residential consumers, which bundles commodity costs with pipeline tariffs and utility distribution charges. [1]
Henry Hub is the simplest natural gas benchmark to value using a bottom-up approach from raw input sources. Because it sits at the center of the transparent, heavily regulated U.S. pipeline network, almost every fundamental input—from wellhead production to storage injections—is reported on a weekly or daily basis by public agencies.
Why Henry Hub Fits Bottom-Up Modeling
Unlike European (TTF) or Asian (JKM) benchmarks—which depend heavily on opaque global LNG shipping schedules, geopolitical shifts, and oil-indexed contract slopes—Henry Hub pricing is driven primarily by domestic physical supply and demand balances.
High Data Granularity. The U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) and interstate pipeline operators publish transparent, high-frequency data on production, storage, and regional flows.
Clear Cost-of-Supply Curve. North American shale basins (like the Permian, Haynesville, and Appalachia) have well-documented breakeven drilling and completion costs, establishing a clear fundamental floor for gas prices.
Direct Power-Burn Substitution. In the U.S. power grid, natural gas competes directly with coal based on transparent heat rates and fuel costs, creating a predictable price ceiling and floor through coal-to-gas switching economics.
Core Raw Inputs for a Bottom-Up Valuation
To build a fundamental bottom-up valuation model for Henry Hub, analysts stack physical supply against physical demand and balance the difference through storage:
Supply Inputs (Bcf/d). Dry gas production volumes by basin, net pipeline imports from Canada, and associated gas output driven by West Texas crude oil drilling.
Domestic Demand Inputs (Bcf/d). Residential and commercial heating demand (driven by Heating Degree Days), industrial consumption, and electric power generation burn (driven by Cooling Degree Days and renewable generation).
Export Sinks (Bcf/d). LNG export terminal feedgas nominations (which are publicly visible via pipeline flow data) and pipeline exports to Mexico.
The Balancing Variable (Storage). The net surplus or deficit from the inputs above dictates weekly injections or withdrawals from underground storage, which is measured directly against the EIA five-year average to determine fair value.
