What you are describing is a Point-in-Time (PIT) Walk-Forward Backtest, which is the gold standard in quantitative energy trading for testing whether raw data can accurately predict forward prices without hindsight bias. By restricting your model on any given historical day to only the exact reports, forecasts, and pipeline flows published up to that moment, you can objectively measure which variables actually predicted the market.
1. Build an "As-Of" Point-in-Time Database
Standard historical datasets are dangerous for backtesting because agencies constantly revise past numbers. To simulate what a trader actually saw on a specific morning, every data point needs two timestamps.
Observation Date. This is the calendar date the physical event happened, such as a specific day of gas production or storage withdrawal.
Release Timestamp. This is the exact date and time the data became public, such as the EIA Weekly Natural Gas Storage Report dropping on Thursday at 10:30 AM EST.
Weather Forecasts vs. Actuals. You must use archived weather model runs (like GFS or ECMWF ensembles) issued on that exact historical morning rather than actual temperatures that were recorded later.
Unrevised EIA Vintages. You must pull historical "vintage" snapshots of production and demand data so your model does not benefit from revisions published months later.
2. Structure the Daily Walk-Forward Loop
To automate this across several months or years, your code will step forward one trading day at a time and rebuild the valuation from scratch.
Freeze the Clock. Pick a historical valuation date \(t\) and filter your entire database so that only rows where release_timestamp <= t are visible.
Calculate the Theoretical Forward Price. Run your bottom-up supply, demand, and storage balance model using those frozen inputs to generate fair values for upcoming contract months (\(T_1, T_2, T_3\)).
Log the Prediction and Actuals. Record your model's predicted price alongside the actual NYMEX forward price trading that day and the final settlement price when the contract eventually expired.
Step Forward and Repeat. Advance the clock to \(t + 1\) day, unlock the newly published data for that day, and run the calculation again.
3. Test Core and Cross-Domain Variables Safely
Once your historical loop runs cleanly, you can systematically plug in new raw inputs and unconventional variables to see if they improve accuracy.
Baseline Fundamental Stack. Start with core drivers like daily pipeline feedgas to LNG terminals, dry gas production estimates, and 14-day Population-Weighted Degree Day forecasts.
Cross-Domain Signals. Layer in unconventional variables such as Mont Belvieu NGL purity prices, regional power grid congestion, coal-to-gas switching spreads, or industrial railcar loadings.
Correlation and Error Scoring. Measure each variable's impact using out-of-sample Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and directional hit rates rather than simple linear correlation alone.
Guard Against Data Snooping. When testing dozens of random or alternative variables, hold back a completely untouched block of historical time (such as the most recent six months) to verify that a discovered correlation is real and not random noise.
